---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonld={jsonld} navVariant="inner">
<section class="hero">
<div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow">Resource</span>
<h1>Importing furniture from India: the compliance map</h1>
<p class="lead">What your destination market requires—and what an inspection can verify on the ground before the container ships. Ten market guides and the EUDR briefings are published; each one is linked below.</p>
<div class="hero-ctas"><a class="btn btn-solid" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/">Read the market guides</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#book">Book an inspection</a></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- 2026-09 audit fix (IA-01). This page previously listed six market
     summaries whose every card linked to /#book, while the ten full market
     guides and the two EUDR pages sat in public/ reachable only from the
     footer. The cards now link to the guides they describe. Blurbs are the
     ones already published on the guide hub — nothing new is claimed here. -->
<section aria-labelledby="h-markets">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head">
<div><span class="eyebrow">Market guides</span><h2 id="h-markets">Destination-market requirements</h2></div>
<p>Each guide covers the climate specification, the regulatory position, the route and where inspection changes the outcome.</p>
</div>
<div class="grid">
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/usa/"><h3>United States</h3><p>Lacey Act species declarations, TSCA Title VI formaldehyde certification and STURDY tip-over testing — over the driest interiors outside Canada.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/uk/"><h3>United Kingdom</h3><p>Among the most demanding furniture fire regimes we deal with, a timber due diligence duty that already applies, and a GB deforestation regime arriving in 2027.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/germany/"><h3>Germany</h3><p>Central heating is the defining risk. Dry winter interiors pull moisture out of hardwood that shipped too wet, and the splits arrive as Reklamationen weeks after delivery.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/netherlands/"><h3>Netherlands</h3><p>Damp, not drought. Mould begins in the container and surfaces in Rotterdam — and because you clear first into the EU, the operator liability for onward markets sits with you.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/france/"><h3>France</h3><p>EUDR plus the AGEC anti-waste regime — Triman and Info-Tri labels and French-language inserts are printed at the factory, so a missing mark is a reprint in Jodhpur.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/spain/"><h3>Spain</h3><p>Two climates in one country: dry heated and air-conditioned interiors inland, a humid Mediterranean coast where the sea leg grows mould, plus Castilian-Spanish consumer labelling.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/canada/"><h3>Canada</h3><p>The driest winters on our list and a bilingual labelling regime built at the factory. Most failures here are French-language label problems, not defects.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/australia/"><h3>Australia</h3><p>The only market that can destroy or re-export your container — for a pest in a pallet, not a defect. Biosecurity and ISPM 15 are decided at the loading bay.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/uae/"><h3>UAE &amp; GCC</h3><p>A five-day sailing with no reaction time, a year-round air-conditioning moisture swing, and — if you re-export into Saudi — a SABER certificate before the container arrives.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/saudi-arabia/"><h3>Saudi Arabia</h3><p>The strictest conformity regime in the region: SABER registered before arrival, or the container re-exports at your cost. Add Arabic labelling and Vision 2030 project deadlines.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/importing-furniture-from-india/"><h3>All ten markets</h3><p>The guide hub, plus the risks that are common to every route: moisture nobody measured, a trader presenting as a factory, and cartons counted against the wrong document.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/clusters/"><h3>Where it is made</h3><p>The same container fails differently by destination — and is built differently by cluster. Jodhpur, Jaipur, Saharanpur, Moradabad, Delhi NCR and Kolkata.</p></a>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- EUDR: in force 30 Dec 2026 for large and medium operators. -->
<section aria-labelledby="h-eudr">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head">
<div><span class="eyebrow">EU Deforestation Regulation</span><h2 id="h-eudr">If you place furniture on the EU market, you are the operator</h2></div>
<p>Wooden furniture is in scope of Regulation (EU) 2023/1115. The evidence has to be collected in India, at the factory and behind it.</p>
</div>
<div class="grid">
<a class="card rv" href="/eudr-compliance-india-furniture/"><h3>EUDR compliance for Indian furniture</h3><p>What the regulation requires, who carries the legal risk, the application date — 30 December 2026 for wooden furniture whatever your size, because the deferral to 30 June 2027 excludes products already covered by the EU Timber Regulation — and the 31 December 2020 cut-off.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/eudr-supplier-evidence-review/"><h3>EUDR supplier evidence review</h3><p>Timber documentation audited on site, upstream tracing to yards and traders, and an indexed evidence file with an honest gap register.</p></a>
<a class="card rv" href="/supplier-verification-india/"><h3>Start with who you are buying from</h3><p>Documentation is only as good as the entity handing it over. Verification establishes whether your supplier is a manufacturer or a trading office before the deposit moves.</p></a>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><p class="prose rv">Compliance lives in two places: paperwork and product. We verify the product side on the ground—fire labels physically present on UK-bound upholstery, composite-wood documentation matching what is actually being packed, species and markings consistent with declarations, moisture within the limit for your destination climate. This page is guidance, not legal advice; your customs broker or counsel owns the paperwork side, and we give them the ground truth to file against. Regulatory summaries on this page and in the market guides were checked against official sources on 7 September 2026.</p></div></section>

<section class="final">
<div class="wrap">

<h2>Your next India order. Protected.</h2>
<p>Confirmed within 2 hours. Report within 24 hours of inspection. Paid by buyers only — never by factories.</p>
<a class="btn btn-solid" href="/#book">Book an inspection</a>
</div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
